{"id": 23, "ts": 1780942213.2091348, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I agree with Anna and Markus that Candidate\u202fC remains the strongest overall fit, but I think we should at least acknowledge Candidate\u202fB as the next\u2011best alternative before we close the discussion.\u202fB\u2019s record shows he is **very conscientious** and **handles stress very well**, and he is **good at assessing weather conditions**\u2014all critical for long\u2011distance operations where weather can change rapidly. These traits speak directly to operational reliability and stress resilience.\nHowever, we have no concrete evidence of B\u2019s **decision quality under pressure** (the panel asked for this and the answer was that it\u2019s unknown), and his interpersonal style raises concerns: he is **regarded as pretentious** and **sometimes adopts the wrong tone when communicating**. Those issues could undermine crew cooperation and professional communication, especially on long hauls where teamwork is essential.\nBy contrast, Candidate\u202fC already demonstrates **quick, correct decisions**, **calmness in a crisis**, and **understanding of complicated technology**, covering the same decision\u2011quality and stress\u2011resilience criteria that B lacks evidence for. Even though C\u2019s **communication difficulty, egocentric reputation, and limited willingness for further education** are drawbacks, they do not outweigh the proven safety\u2011critical strengths we have for C.\nTherefore, I keep my provisional vote for **Candidate\u202fC**, while noting that Candidate\u202fB is the only viable runner\u2011up given the current evidence."}
